@meta
id: 05-14
title: Word motions ignore line ends
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-e, word-counts
par: 19
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl-Right stops dead at the end of the line
open: refund.types.ts

@brief
Every word motion runs off the end of one line and onto the start of the
next. `w` on the last word of a line lands on the first word of the line
below; `e` and `b` do the same in their own directions. The file is one
long stream of words to them, and the newlines are just more whitespace.

VS Code does not do this — Ctrl-Right parks at the end of the line and
you press it again to move on. That difference is why a count can cross
lines here and cannot there.

Two fields on `RefundInput` are optional now. Both `amount` and `reason`
should carry a `?`:

    amount?: number;
    reason?: string;

@keys
| `e` | end of the current or next word, across line ends |
| `4e` | four of those |
| `a` | append after the cursor |
| `2j` | down two lines |

@hints
The two edits are `ea?` twice. The interesting part is getting from the
first one to the second.
--
After the first `?`, keep pressing `e` and watch it walk off the end of
the line onto `reason` without you touching `j`.
--
`2j` `ea?` `Esc`, then `4e` — over `:`, `string`, `;` and onto the end of
`reason` — then `a?` `Esc` `:wq`.

@solution
    2j  ea?<Esc>  4e  a?<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes for both fields, and no vertical motion at all after
the first jump. `4e` crossed a line boundary as if it were a space.

This is worth internalising because it changes how you aim. For anything
within a line or two, you do not need to move down and then across. You
count word-ends and go, which is one motion instead of two and no chance
of landing in the wrong column.

The counterweight: because they run on, an over-large count silently
carries you into the next function. Word motions are for short hops. Once
the target is more than a line or two away, you want `}`, a search or a
line jump, and chapters six, seven and twelve hand you all three.

@start refund.types.ts
export type RefundInput = {
  orderId: string;
  amount: number;
  reason: string;
};

@expected refund.types.ts
export type RefundInput = {
  orderId: string;
  amount?: number;
  reason?: string;
};
